-- $ProjectHeader: use 0.393 Wed, 16 May 2007 14:10:28 +0200 opti $
--
-- Ported from this repo's own bundled USE example
-- use-core/src/main/resources/examples/Others/Tree/Tree.use (see Info.txt
-- alongside it), for the KK-ModelValidator plugin. Class, association and
-- the four operation bodies below (childPlus1/childPlus2/
-- childPlusOnNodeSet/childPlus3) are copied verbatim from that file, only
-- reformatted/commented. This is a deliberately DIFFERENT recursive-
-- structure demonstration than 05-Genealogy: Genealogy's Person/Parenthood
-- model states its cycle-freedom invariant directly with OCL's *built-in*
-- closure() iterator; Tree.use instead defines its own *user-level
-- recursive OCL operations* (an operation whose body calls itself, directly
-- or through another operation) to compute the same kind of transitive
-- reachability. That difference turns out to matter a great deal for this
-- plugin -- see the two findings below, both confirmed by actually running
-- the plugin, not assumed from reading its source.
--
-- FINDING 1 (transform-time rejection, confirmed against the plugin's own
-- source and by actually running it -- see validate.cmd's
-- recursiveOpProbe section):
-- org.tzi.use.kodkod.transform.ocl.OperationExpressionVisitor keeps a
-- global OperationStack (org.tzi.use.kodkod.transform.OperationStack) of
-- operations currently being inlined while transforming an OCL invariant to
-- a Kodkod formula. If the same MOperation is encountered twice on that
-- stack -- i.e. the operation calls itself, directly or via another
-- operation -- the transform throws
-- TransformationException("Operation '<name>' is recursive and thereby
-- cannot be transformed."). All four operations below are recursive in
-- exactly this sense (childPlus1 calls itself inside collect(); childPlus2
-- delegates to childPlusOnNodeSet, which calls itself; childPlus3 avoids
-- operation-level recursion by using `iterate` instead, but its use of
-- `iterate` over TreeNode.allInstances() is untested by this example and
-- not relied upon below). So the ORIGINAL upstream invariant --
--   context TreeNode inv AcyclicParentship:
--     not(self.childPlus2()->includes(self))
-- -- can never be handed to Kodkod as-is by this plugin build. It is kept
-- below, verbatim, as AcyclicParentshipRecursive -- its active/inactive
-- toggle is set to `inactive` in every Tree.properties section except
-- recursiveOpProbe, a dedicated, deliberately tiny probe section whose
-- whole point is to set it `active` instead (see validate.cmd). Note
-- this toggle does not actually change whether the transform is
-- ATTEMPTED (confirmed: it is attempted, and fails, regardless -- see
-- Tree.properties' header); it only controls whether a successfully
-- transformed invariant's formula would have been asserted, had
-- transformation succeeded.
--
-- FINDING 2 (a genuine, confirmed authoring quirk in the ORIGINAL upstream
-- invariant, independent of Finding 1 and independent of Kodkod entirely --
-- reproduced with plain USE, no plugin involved at all): childPlus2() is
-- defined as self.childPlusOnNodeSet(Set{self}) -- the accumulator set
-- handed to the very first call is seeded with `self` itself, and every
-- recursive step only ever *unions into* that accumulator, never removes
-- from it. So self is a member of self.childPlus2()'s result from the very
-- first line of the recursion, for EVERY TreeNode, in EVERY instance --
-- including instances with no cycle whatsoever. That makes
-- "not(self.childPlus2()->includes(self))" evaluate to `false` for every
-- TreeNode in every non-empty population, acyclic or not: it is not
-- actually an acyclicity check as shipped. Confirmed directly against the
-- untouched upstream Tree.use, no changes of any kind:
--   * Tree.cmd's own 9-node graph (which does contain a real cycle):
--     `check -v` -> "checked 1 invariant in 0.089s, 1 failure."
--   * Tree2.cmd's 2-node mutual cycle (the one case Info.txt explicitly
--     calls out as "the invariant evaluates to false"): also 1 failure.
--   * A FRESH, perfectly acyclic 2-node instance (one root, one child,
--     built from scratch, no cycle anywhere): STILL "1 failure" -- the
--     exact same result as the two cyclic cases above. So Info.txt's
--     framing (implying Tree2.cmd is the interesting/different case) does
--     not hold up: the invariant fails identically whether or not the
--     instance actually has a cycle. This is evidently a 2007-era authoring
--     slip in the bundled example (the seed should presumably have been
--     Set{} or the accumulator should track *strict* descendants), not
--     something introduced by this port.
--
-- Given both findings, the invariant this example actually hands to Kodkod
-- (AcyclicParentshipClosure below) restates the SAME intended property --
-- "a node is never its own descendant" -- the same way 05-Genealogy states
-- its own acyclicParenthood invariant: with closure() over the child role,
-- which this plugin does support (Genealogy's own
-- "recursive-association" feature). It is intentionally NOT a rename of
-- the broken original; it is a from-scratch, independently-correct
-- statement of the property Info.txt describes ("does not allow cycles"),
-- checked genuinely both ways below: SATISFIABLE on a hand-built acyclic
-- tree (Tree.properties [tree]), and TRIVIALLY_UNSATISFIABLE the moment a
-- cycle is forced into a same-shaped instance ([forcedCycle]).
--
-- Info.txt also describes multiplicity, not an extra invariant: "a parent
-- may have several children but only one parent or no parent, if it is the
-- root node" is exactly the `TreeNode [0..1] role parent` bound below, not
-- a class invariant -- Tree.use declares no explicit "single root" /
-- "fully connected" invariant anywhere, and none is added here either. The
-- [tree] section below happens to reconstruct a single-rooted, fully
-- connected instance (verified with a query in query.cmd), but that is a
-- property of the chosen population/link bounds (TreeNode_max = 7,
-- Parentship_max = 6 -- with `parent` capped at 0..1 per node, exactly
-- n-1 links among n nodes forces exactly one parentless node), not of an
-- extra invariant.

model Tree

class TreeNode
attributes
  name : String
operations
  -- Kept verbatim from upstream for documentation/fidelity. Never invoked
  -- from any constraint below (see Finding 1 above) -- their presence here
  -- does not affect -validate at all, since the plugin only ever attempts
  -- to transform an operation body when a constraint actually calls it.
  -- They remain perfectly usable through the ordinary (non-Kodkod) OCL
  -- interpreter, e.g. from a plain `?` query against a concrete object
  -- diagram -- query.cmd demonstrates exactly that, post-validate.

  -- works for dags
  childPlus1():Set(TreeNode) =
    Set{self}->union(self.child)->union(self.child->collect(tn | tn.childPlus1())->flatten->asSet)
  -- works for graphs
  childPlus2():Set(TreeNode) =
    self.childPlusOnNodeSet(Set{self})
  childPlusOnNodeSet(aTreeNodeSet:Set(TreeNode)):Set(TreeNode) =
    if aTreeNodeSet->includesAll(self.child) then
      aTreeNodeSet
    else
      self.child->collect(tn |
        tn.childPlusOnNodeSet(aTreeNodeSet->union(Set{tn})->union(self.child)))->flatten->asSet
    endif
  childPlus3():Set(TreeNode) =
    TreeNode.allInstances->iterate(tn1:TreeNode; result:Set(TreeNode)=Set{self} |
      result->union(result->collect(tn2 | tn2.child)->flatten->asSet))
end

association Parentship between
  TreeNode [0..1] role parent
  TreeNode [0..*] role child
end

constraints

-- Verbatim upstream invariant (see Finding 1 and Finding 2 above). Its
-- own active/inactive toggle (`TreeNode_AcyclicParentshipRecursive`) is
-- `inactive` in every .properties section except recursiveOpProbe's,
-- which sets it `active` purely as a minimal, isolated repro of the
-- transform-time recursion rejection -- the ERROR line itself fires
-- regardless of this toggle (see FINDING 1 above).
context self:TreeNode inv AcyclicParentshipRecursive:
  not(self.childPlus2()->includes(self))

-- Working restatement actually solved for by Tree.properties'
-- [tree]/[forcedCycle] sections: self is never its own descendant,
-- computed via closure() (supported -- see 05-Genealogy's
-- acyclicParenthood) instead of a self-recursive user operation.
context self:TreeNode inv AcyclicParentshipClosure:
  self.child->closure(child)->excludes(self)
